  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 211 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nAn upper level trough is centered over the Great Lakes region \nand extends over the Midwest. An upper level ridge is centered \nover Arizona and New Mexico and extends northward to Montana. \nNebraska and Kansas are in between these 2 features. A surface \nhigh over the northern Plains extends southward to Nebraska and \nKansas. Winds across south central and central Nebraska and \nnorth central Kansas are mostly out of the east to north at \naround 5 to 10 MPH. High temperatures today will mainly be in \nthe 80s with mostly sunny to partly cloudy skies this afternoon.\n\nAn upper level shortwave will move over the area tonight which\nwill bring increased chances (around 30% to 40%) of showers and\nthunderstorms mainly for areas west of the Tri-Cities. Although,\nareas as far west as Highway 281 will have a 15% to 25% chance\nof showers and storms late tonight. While severe weather is\ngenerally not expected with these storms, it cannot be ruled out\neither (around a 20% chance). Hail up to quarter size and wind\ngusts up to 60 MPH will be the main threats with these storms.Low\ntemperatures tonight will mainly be in the low to mid 60s. \n\nThe showers and storms are expected to continue through the morning\nhours (15% to around 35% chance) on Thursday and into the \nafternoon. Most locations along and east of Highway 81 are \nexpected to remain dry. High temperatures on Thursday will be in\nthe 80s. There are some indications of some showers and storms \ndeveloping across mainly western portions of the forecast area \n(similar areas as the previous night) late Thursday night. \n\nThe previously mentioned upper ridge to the west will shift\neastward by Friday through the weekend. The ridge will have more\nimpact over south central and central Nebraska and north central\nKansas resulting in drier and warmer conditions. The warmest 2\ndays appear to be Saturday and Sunday with temperatures possibly\nranging from the mid 80s to around 100 degrees. Temperatures\ncool slightly during the early to middle part of next week with\nthe upper ridge becoming more centered over the southeast part\nof the country. Various upper disturbances may bring more rain\nand storms to portions of the area early to mid next week.",
